# Domain Profile: Maker / Physical-World Engineering [DOMAIN:MKR]

> Encodes the knowledge of a hardware and electronics workspace: the projects underway, the boards and components on hand, how they are wired and powered, and the reusable patterns for bringing unfamiliar hardware to life. Unlike a pure software domain, its assertions bind to physical objects — pins, voltages, buses, and enclosures — where a wrong value is not a bug but a burned board.

> Maker is a cross-cutting layer as much as a knowledge domain: its identity, tooling, and engineering patterns apply across whatever project is active, and are loaded alongside project-specific corpora rather than in isolation.

---

## 1. Domain Identity

| Attribute | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| Namespace | MKR |
| Sub-Namespaces | MKR.INV (inventory: boards, components), MKR.PROJ (projects), MKR.PAT (bring-up and debugging patterns) |
| Location | Project-local, plus a shared workspace-context corpus |
| Conventions File | This profile |
| Maturity | Reference profile — generic, adopt and extend per workspace |

---

## 2. Structure

### 2.1 Inventory (MKR.INV)

The catalog of physical things: microcontrollers, single-board computers, sensors, expanders, and their electrical characteristics. Inventory nodes are the nouns other layers refer to. Each records the physical facts a project needs to design against — voltage levels, bus addresses, pin counts, current draw.

### 2.2 Projects (MKR.PROJ)

The builds: what is being made, from which inventory parts, in what state. Project nodes bind a goal to a set of components, a wiring topology, and a running record of what was learned. A project references inventory; it does not duplicate it.

### 2.3 Patterns (MKR.PAT)

The durable, reusable engineering knowledge extracted from projects: how to bring up an unknown board, how to bisect a hardware fault, how to bring an unresponsive device onto a bus. Pattern nodes carry a problem statement, an ordered strategy, and the invariant that makes the strategy safe.

---

## 3. Foundational Design Decisions

### 3.1 DD-MKR-1: Assertions Bind to Physical Objects

- decision: Inventory and wiring nodes carry the electrical and physical facts of real objects (voltage, current, pin, bus address, footprint). These are not documentation; they are the design constraints a project must respect.
- rationale: In physical-world engineering a wrong value has a physical consequence. Encoding voltage levels, pin assignments, and current budgets as first-class properties lets a check catch a 5V signal on a 3.3V pin before the smoke.
- status: foundational

### 3.2 DD-MKR-2: Projects Reference Inventory, Not Copy It

- decision: A project cites inventory nodes by id (`mkr.USES`) rather than restating a component's characteristics. A component's electrical facts live in exactly one place.
- rationale: The same board appears in many projects. Duplicating its pinout invites drift — two projects disagreeing about the same chip. One authoritative inventory node prevents it.
- status: foundational

### 3.3 DD-MKR-3: Patterns Carry Their Invariant

- decision: Every MKR.PAT node records not just a `strategy` but the `invariant` that must hold for the strategy to be safe. A pattern without its invariant fails linting.
- rationale: A bring-up sequence is only safe under conditions (power off before rewiring; confirm ground before signal). The invariant is the load-bearing part; the steps are its consequence. Recording only the steps loses why they are ordered as they are.
- status: foundational

### 3.4 DD-MKR-4: Physical Topology Uses Dedicated Edge Types

- decision: Physical relationships (wired-to, powered-by, communicates-via, mounted-on) are distinct edge types, not generic dependencies.
- rationale: "Component A depends on component B" is too coarse for hardware. Whether B powers A, signals A over I2C, or is merely bolted to the same chassis changes what a fault in B does to A. Distinct edges make blast-radius queries meaningful.
- status: foundational

### 3.5 DD-MKR-5: Lessons Are Attributed to the Project That Taught Them

- decision: Pattern nodes carry a `learned-from` reference to the project during which the lesson was earned.
- rationale: A debugging pattern is more trustworthy when its provenance is visible — a future builder can read the originating project for the full context behind the generalized rule.
- status: foundational

---

## 4. ID Prefix Registry

### 4.1 Inventory Prefixes (MKR.INV)

| Prefix | Usage | Example |
|--------|-------|---------|
| BOARD- | Microcontroller / single-board computer | [BOARD-MCU-1], [BOARD-SBC-1] |
| COMP- | Component catalog entry | [COMP-I2C-EXPANDER], [COMP-LED-DRIVER] |
| SCHEMA-COMPONENT | Component schema definition | [SCHEMA-COMPONENT] |
| INFRA- | Fixed infrastructure (host, network, power supply) | [INFRA-BENCH-PSU], [INFRA-HOST-1] |

### 4.2 Project Prefixes (MKR.PROJ)

| Prefix | Usage | Example |
|--------|-------|---------|
| PROJ- | Project node | [PROJ-KIOSK], [PROJ-SENSOR-NODE] |
| SCHEMA-PROJECT | Project schema definition | [SCHEMA-PROJECT] |
| WIRE- | Wiring / connection record | [WIRE-I2C-BUS-1], [WIRE-POWER-RAIL] |

### 4.3 Pattern Prefixes (MKR.PAT)

| Prefix | Usage | Example |
|--------|-------|---------|
| PAT-HW- | Hardware bring-up pattern | [PAT-HW-FIRST-BOOT], [PAT-HW-BUS-SCAN] |
| PAT-DEBUG- | Hardware debugging pattern | [PAT-DEBUG-MYSTERY-BOARD] |
| ML-R | Maker-log rule / heuristic | [ML-R-LABEL-EVERYTHING] |

### 4.4 Workspace-Context Prefixes

| Prefix | Usage | Example |
|--------|-------|---------|
| ID- | Workspace / operator identity | [ID-OPERATOR] |
| BEH- | Working-style / collaboration guidance | [BEH-VERIFY-BEFORE-POWER] |

---

## 5. Property Vocabulary

### 5.1 Electrical and Physical Properties (MKR.INV)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| voltage | string | Operating / logic voltage (e.g. 3.3V, 5V) |
| current-draw | string | Typical / max current draw |
| bus | enum | i2c, spi, uart, can, 1-wire, gpio |
| bus-address | string | Device address on its bus (e.g. I2C 0x20) |
| pins | number | Usable I/O pin count |
| pinout-ref | reference | Node or document holding the full pin map |
| footprint | string | Physical package / mounting footprint |
| limitations | string | Known electrical or firmware limitations |

### 5.2 Project Properties (MKR.PROJ)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| goal | string | What the project is meant to accomplish |
| state | enum | planned, breadboard, assembled, deployed, retired |
| uses | list | Inventory node ids this project builds from |
| deploy-location | string | Where the finished build lives |
| services | list | Software services the build runs, if any |

### 5.3 Pattern Properties (MKR.PAT)

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| problem | string | The recurring problem the pattern addresses |
| strategy | string | The resolution approach |
| sequence | list | Ordered steps to execute the strategy |
| invariant | string | The condition that must hold for the strategy to be safe |
| learned-from | reference | Project node that first taught this lesson |

---

## 6. Domain-Specific Edge Types

| Type | Layer | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| mkr.USES | entity | A project is built from an inventory component |
| mkr.WIRED_TO | entity | One component is physically connected to another |
| mkr.POWERED_BY | causal | A component draws power from a source or rail |
| mkr.COMMUNICATES_VIA | semantic | Two components exchange data over a named bus/protocol |
| mkr.MOUNTED_ON | entity | A component is physically mounted on a board or chassis |
| mkr.LEARNED_FROM | temporal | A pattern was extracted from a specific project |
| mkr.CONSTRAINS | causal | A physical characteristic constrains a design choice |

---

## 7. Document Type Tags

| Tag | Description |
|-----|-------------|
| INVENTORY | Board / component catalog document |
| PROJECT | Project definition and running record |
| PATTERN | Reusable bring-up or debugging pattern |
| WIRING | Connection and power-topology record |
| PROFILE | Workspace / operator context |
| GUIDE | Working-style or engineering-log guidance |

---

## 8. Confidence and State

Inventory nodes are typically high-confidence (they restate a datasheet or a measured value) and change only when a part is replaced or a measurement is corrected. Project nodes carry a `state` rather than a numeric confidence, because a build's truth is its physical stage, not a probability. Pattern nodes gain implicit confidence as their `learned-from` list grows — a heuristic confirmed across several projects is more trustworthy than one seen once.

---

## 9. Temporal Semantics

### 9.1 Project State Progression

A project moves `planned → breadboard → assembled → deployed → retired`. Each transition is a durable fact; a retired project's nodes are kept so its wiring and lessons remain queryable.

### 9.2 Inventory Correction

When a measured electrical characteristic is corrected, the inventory node is updated in place and the prior value is captured in a DELTA record — physical facts do not accumulate versions the way decisions do, but the correction history matters when a downstream build assumed the old value.

---

## 10. Traversal Policy

```yaml
traversal:
  default_entry_points: ["PROJ-INDEX"]
  primary_layers: [entity, causal]
  focus_areas:
    projects:
      entry: ["PROJ-INDEX"]
      layers: [entity, causal]
      keywords: ["project", "build", "state", "deploy"]
    inventory:
      entry: ["COMP-INDEX", "BOARD-INDEX"]
      layers: [entity, semantic]
      keywords: ["component", "board", "part", "voltage", "pinout", "bus"]
    wiring:
      entry: ["WIRE-INDEX"]
      layers: [entity, causal]
      keywords: ["wiring", "power", "connection", "i2c", "spi", "topology"]
    patterns:
      entry: ["PAT-INDEX"]
      layers: [causal, temporal]
      keywords: ["pattern", "bring-up", "debug", "mystery", "sequence", "invariant"]
    workspace:
      entry: ["ID-OPERATOR"]
      layers: [semantic]
      keywords: ["operator", "workspace", "style", "guidance", "collaboration"]
```

---

## 11. Corpus Structure

```
<workspace>/
├── context/                           # Cross-cutting workspace layer
│   ├── 00-operator-profile.lts        # ID-, BEH- nodes
│   └── 01-working-style.lts           # BEH- nodes
├── inventory/                         # MKR.INV
│   ├── 10-boards.lts                  # BOARD- nodes
│   ├── 11-components.lts              # COMP- nodes (with electrical properties)
│   └── 12-infrastructure.lts          # INFRA- nodes
├── projects/                          # MKR.PROJ
│   └── <project>/
│       ├── 20-project.lts             # PROJ- node
│       └── 21-wiring.lts              # WIRE- nodes
├── patterns/                          # MKR.PAT
│   ├── 30-bringup-patterns.lts        # PAT-HW- nodes
│   └── 31-debug-patterns.lts          # PAT-DEBUG- nodes
├── events/
│   └── quality.jsonl                  # Quality event stream
└── deltas/
    └── DELTA-{date}-{short-sha}.lts
```

---

## 12. Cross-Cutting Nature

Maker functions as **workspace context** as well as a knowledge domain. Operator identity and working-style guidance apply regardless of which project is active, so the `context/` corpus is loaded alongside any project rather than owned by one. This is why the domain carries an identity/behavior prefix set (`ID-`, `BEH-`) that a pure knowledge domain would not: the profile describes not only the hardware but the conventions of the person building it.

